 "briefSummary": "This study is being conducted to characterize the safety, tolerability, and efficacy of AP306 at fixed doses in adults with hyperphosphatemia receiving maintenance hemodialysis. An optional open-label extension (OLE) phase (US only) will evaluate the long-term safety, tolerability, and serum phosphate-lowering effect of AP306 for up to 52 weeks in eligible participants who complete the double-blind phase.",
 "detailedDescription": "Hyperphosphatemia, one of the most common complications of advanced chronic kidney disease, becomes increasingly prevalent as kidney function declines and is found almost universally in patients with end-stage kidney disease requiring dialysis. Hyperphosphatemia is an independent risk factor for cardiovascular outcomes, fractures, and mortality in patients with chronic kidney disease, especially in patients receiving dialysis.\n\nAP306 is a pan-phosphate transporter inhibitor that may stop phosphate absorption in the gut, controlling hyperphosphatemia.\n\nThe first phase of this study is a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, study to characterize the safety, tolerability, and efficacy of AP306 given daily for 8 weeks at fixed doses in adults with hyperphosphatemia receiving maintenance hemodialysis.\n\nUpon completion of the double-blind phase (including the 4-week follow-up period), eligible participants in the US may enroll in an optional open-label extension (OLE) phase consisting of up to 52 weeks of open-label treatment with AP306 (dose adjusted every 4 weeks or as clinically indicated based on serum phosphate levels), followed by a 4-week follow-up period.",
